# Discriminative compression pilot v1

## Hypothesis

On the native metadata-stripped 20 Newsgroups task, simple example compression
(uniform one-pass reservoir subsets) or standard feature compression
(`TruncatedSVD`) may provide a genuine end-to-end CPU time / macro-F1 tradeoff
relative to a strong full sparse Ridge classifier.  A component is locally
promising only if its quality loss is at most 0.03 absolute macro-F1 and its
method-specific wall time is at most 75% of the full sparse baseline.

This is a baseline gate, not a claim for a new coreset, streaming statistic, or
low-rank algorithm.

## Data and leakage controls

- Native `subset="train"` and `subset="test"`, `shuffle=False`.
- Remove `("headers", "footers", "quotes")` in both subsets.
- All 20 native classes.
- Loader cache: `.deps/sklearn_data`, excluded from checkpoints.
- TF-IDF settings exactly as frozen by the source audit:
  `sublinear_tf=True, max_df=0.5, min_df=5, stop_words="english"`.
- Every vectorizer is fitted only on that method's retained training documents;
  the native test text is transform-only.

## Methods

1. `full_sparse`: full training text, sparse TF-IDF, `RidgeClassifier(alpha=1,
   solver="sparse_cg", tol=1e-2)`.
2. `reservoir_25` and `reservoir_50`: a standard one-pass uniform reservoir of
   25% or 50% of the native training sequence, seeds 19, 71, and 211.  Each
   method refits its own TF-IDF and the same sparse Ridge objective.  Class
   counts are retained to diagnose accidental coverage loss.
3. `svd_128` and `svd_256`: full sparse TF-IDF, randomized `TruncatedSVD` with
   five power iterations and seed 19, followed by the same Ridge objective with
   a dense-compatible LSQR solver.  SVD fit and both transforms are counted.

No hyperparameter is selected using test performance.  These fixed points test
whether there is enough signal to justify a later method-specific study.

## Metrics and costs

- Primary quality: macro-F1; secondary: accuracy.
- Record sample counts, class counts, sparse shape/nnz, package versions, and
  every fixed parameter.
- Record data load/download wall time separately.
- Method wall includes reservoir selection where applicable, vectorizer fit and
  both transforms, SVD construction/transforms where applicable, classifier
  fit, and prediction.
- Record process `ru_maxrss` after every method as a cumulative upper bound and
  final peak RSS.  The outer `cpu_guard.py` enforces the 4 GiB address-space
  limit and single-thread environment.

## Predictions and decisions

- Full sparse Ridge is expected to set the strongest quality/time reference.
- A 50% reservoir may approach full macro-F1 while reducing vectorization and
  fitting cost; 25% should be faster but may exceed the 0.03 loss gate.
- SVD-128/256 may preserve much of the quality but is expected to lose the time
  gate once decomposition cost is counted.
- If no compressed method satisfies both gates, record a scoped negative and do
  not build a new coreset/low-rank candidate on this benchmark.  The next model
  application question should then isolate a genuine streaming constraint
  (single-pass hashed features and bounded state) rather than relabel batch
  subsampling as streaming.
- Any leakage, missing class, memory failure, or inability to reproduce the
  native split invalidates the corresponding result and triggers audit, not a
  favorable claim.

